---
/*
  /constellations — the overview now lives at `/` (home IS the whole sky,
  2026-07). This alias survives as a courtesy redirect for anything that
  learned the old address.

  ⚠ IT IS A HAND-BUILT `Response` SO THE REDIRECT CAN BE CACHED, and the two
  halves of that are separate facts. A page that RETURNS a `Response`
  short-circuits the render, so `Astro.response.headers` never reaches the wire
  — the header has to sit on the object actually being returned, which
  `Astro.redirect()` builds out of reach. Uncached, every stale bookmark and
  every crawler still holding the old address costs a function invocation to be
  told something that is true forever.

  A year is safe here in a way it would not be on the 302s the dev-gated benches
  return: 301 already claims permanence, and this URL is not coming back.
  `max-age` is deliberately the shorter of the two — a CDN entry can be purged
  on a deploy and a reader's browser cannot, so the promise made to the edge can
  be bolder than the one made to a stranger's disk.
*/
return new Response(null, {
  status: 301,
  headers: {
    Location: '/',
    'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=31536000',
  },
});
---
